timestamp=2026-11-09T09:01:11.915Z level=INFO run=7904429e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So invalid/inconsistent data exists. The task says: \"If any invalid or inconsistent data are found in the input,\n# they should be corrected where possible based on available data, or otherwise excluded from the final results.\"\n# We need to decide: how to correct? Also hours*rate + bonuses should sum to total salary? But recorded values often don't match.\n# Also \"total monthly salary for each employee for every month. The total salary is the sum of:\n# (total hours worked in the month × hourly rate) + any bonuses earned for completed tasks in that month.\"\n# So the definition is clear - compute it from hours, rate, and bonuses from tasks. Not necessarily from recorded salary_amount/bonus if they are inconsistent.\n# We should use the correct formula. Correct inconsistencies where possible (e.g. fix task list parsing, fix employee_id typos), and exclude truly invalid/unrecoverable entries.\n# Also negative bonuses are invalid; zero hours maybe exclude that entry from salary calculation? Or treat hours as 0?\n# Also need to know billing month and start day. \"For each month, the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week inherited from the dataset's earliest record. On that day, employees are required to appear at the workplace to collect their salary.\"\n# So billing periods are month-long periods that all start on the same weekday as the earliest record's weekday. That means each billing month is defined by that anchor weekday?\n# e.g. if earliest record is 2025-03-04 (Wed?), then billing month starts on Wednesday of each month? Or the billing period is structured such that every month’s billing cycle starts on that same weekday? Need to interpret: \"the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week inherited from the dataset's earliest record\"\n# Also \"For each month...\" - probably group by calendar month? But billing period might not align exactly to calendar month boundaries depending on start day. Or maybe the billing period is monthly and anchored to that weekday - like pay period. But easier to think: group entries into billing months. The billing month boundaries are determined by the anchor day (weekday of earliest record).\n# That’s important for assigning work to the correct billing month.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
