timestamp=2026-11-09T09:01:28.743Z level=INFO run=7904429e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So billing period starts on Tuesdays. That means each billing month is defined as periods starting on Tuesday and ending on the following Monday (or the cycle is weekly-like but billed monthly)? Or more precisely: \"For each month, the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week inherited from the dataset's earliest record.\" Maybe each month’s billing cycle starts on that weekday. So for example, billing month for March 2025 starts on Tuesday, March 4, 2025 (earliest record date) - and that defines the billing month anchor? Or the billing month is the month whose billing period begins on that weekday.\n# Alternatively, think of how to assign each work entry (by date) to which \"billing month\" it belongs. Since we need \"total monthly salary for each employee for every month\", we must group by billing month. The billing period starts on that anchor weekday each month. So billing month boundaries are: from Tuesday of that month (or the Tuesday that starts that billing period) to the Monday before the next Tuesday (or similar cycle)? Or simply, for each calendar month, the billing period is considered to start on that weekday of that month? Or perhaps the billing cycle is monthly but aligned to the anchor weekday.\n# Another interpretation: the earliest record is on some Tuesday. That Tuesday is the start of a billing period. All billing periods across all months start on Tuesdays (same weekday). So billing periods are Tuesday-to-Tuesday (weekly) cycles? Or monthly cycles starting on Tuesdays - e.g., each month, billing starts on the Tuesday of that month (or the first Tuesday, or the Tuesday that falls on/after certain day). But with only 4 months of data (Mar–Jun 2025), we need to understand how to bucket.\n# Also look at the actual data distribution by month when grouped by calendar month vs by the anchor.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
