timestamp=2026-11-09T09:01:35.371Z level=INFO run=7904429e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\nimport calendar\n\n# earliest is 2025-03-04, Tuesday (weekday 1)\nanchor_weekday = 1  # Tuesday\n# How to define billing months? Maybe the billing month is identified by which month the billing period belongs to - or group by (year, month) of the period's start? Or alternatively, assign each date to a billing period that starts on anchor_weekday.\n# For each date, find which billing period it belongs to. The billing period starts on Tuesdays.\n# So billing period boundary is every Tuesday. That gives weekly billing periods. But the question says \"total monthly salary for each employee for every month\" - \"monthly salary\" suggests grouping by month (billing month).\n# Another interpretation: for each month, the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week (Tuesday) - meaning in that month, the payroll/billing period starts on Tuesday. Maybe the billing month is the calendar month, and the billing period within the month starts on that weekday - but periods might span months? Unusual. Or maybe the anchor is just to say: on that day (Tuesday), salary is collected - and \"for each month...\" means we group by calendar month, and the collection day is Tuesday (of payday)? That’s possible. Also earliest record is in March 2025. Let us look at all dates and see if grouping by calendar month makes sense given the data range (Mar–Jun).\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
