timestamp=2026-11-09T09:01:51.403Z level=INFO run=7904429e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# But let us also think differently. If billing period starts on Tuesdays, then each billing period is a cycle starting Tuesday. A \"monthly\" billing period aligned to Tuesday would mean each period is 4 weeks long starting on Tuesday? Or maybe the billing month is the period from one Tuesday to the next Tuesday that crosses month boundaries in some way? Or perhaps the billing month is identified by the month in which that period ends or starts. With earliest record on 2025-03-04 (a Tuesday), that could be the start of March's billing period. The next month's billing period would start on the Tuesday of April with same alignment? e.g., first Tuesday of April etc. Or the Tuesday closest to month start? Or just the Tuesday that is the anchor - but \"for each month, the billing period...\" maybe each month has a billing period starting on that weekday. Let us look at what other dates fall on Tuesdays around those months.\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\n\n# Get all Tuesdays in Mar-Jun 2025\nfor year, month in [(2025,3),(2025,4),(2025,5),(2025,6)]:\n    # find all Tuesdays\n    import calendar\n    c = calendar.Calendar()\n    for day in c.itermonthdates(year, month):\n        if day.month == month and day.weekday() == 1:  # Tuesday, weekday 1 in Python? Monday 0, so Tue is 1\n            print(day)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
