timestamp=2026-11-09T09:02:11.900Z level=INFO run=7904429e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Earliest record is 2025-03-04 (first Tuesday in March in this list). So billing periods might be aligned to these Tuesday starts - e.g. billing period March runs from 2025-03-04 (Tuesday) to before 2025-04-01 Tuesday? Then entries between 2025-03-04 inclusive and before 2025-04-01 would belong to that billing month/period. That’s plausible. Let us check how many entries fall in each such period.\nfrom datetime import datetime\nimport csv\n\nperiods = []\n# define periods\nboundaries = [\n    ('2025-03-04', '2025-04-01'),  # Mar billing period starting 2025-03-04 (Tue), next Tue 2025-04-01\n    ('2025-04-01', '2025-05-06'), # but 2025-05-06 is next Tue after 2025-04-29? 2025-04-29 is Tue, next Tue is 2025-05-06\n    ('2025-05-06', '2025-06-03'),\n    ('2025-06-03', '2025-07-01'),\n]\n# better: consecutive Tuesdays as boundaries\ntuesdays = [\n    '2025-03-04', '2025-04-01', '2025-05-06', '2025-06-03', '2025-07-01'\n]\n# but let us see exact Tue sequence more carefully\n# Mar 4,11,18,25; Apr 1,8,15,22,29; May 6,13,20,27; Jun 3,10,17,24\ntuesdays = [\n    datetime(2025,3,4),\n    datetime(2025,4,1),\n    datetime(2025,5,6),\n    datetime(2025,6,3),\n    datetime(2025,7,1),\n]\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
